Yes — curse of knowledge recursively applied can become a route toward wisdom, but recursion alone isn’t enough.

The ordinary curse of knowledge is roughly:

K_1:\quad \text{I know }X
\rightarrow
\text{I can no longer easily reconstruct what not-knowing }X\text{ felt like}.

You’ve compressed away the developmental path. The expert sees the finished building and forgets the scaffolding.

Now recurse it:

K_2:\quad
\text{I know that my knowing changes how I imagine your not-knowing}.

That creates a second observer:

\text{expert}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{novice}}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{expert's distortion of novice}}.

And if you can then deliberately reconstruct the path:

\boxed{
\text{Know}
\rightarrow
\text{Forget-the-shortcut temporarily}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruct}
\rightarrow
\text{teach}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
}

then yes, we’re getting close to wisdom.

I’d refine it to:

\boxed{
\text{Wisdom}
\approx
\text{knowledge}
+
\text{knowledge of the limits produced by knowing}
+
\text{ability to change perspective}
+
\text{judgment about when to use which one}.
}

So:

The recurse of knowledge can break the curse of knowledge.

Nice little linguistic involution there. ;)

And that connects directly to:

Not odd one out, odd one up.

That is genuinely useful.

Normally anomaly detection does:

\{A,A,A,B,A\}
\rightarrow
B=\text{reject / outlier}.

Odd One Up asks instead:

\boxed{
B
\rightarrow
\text{Does this anomaly require a higher-order category that explains both }A\text{ and }B?
}

Rather than exile the anomaly, promote the representation.

For example:

\text{bird, bird, bird, bat}

Odd-one-out says bat.

Odd-one-up asks:

What category makes the difference informative?

Flying animals.

Wing structures.

Convergent evolution.

Navigation.

Echolocation versus vision.

Suddenly the “wrong” member becomes the ladder to another abstraction level.

Formally:

\boxed{
\operatorname{ODD\_ONE\_UP}(x,D)
=
\text{search for representation }R'
\text{ where }x\text{ becomes explanatory rather than erroneous}.
}

That’s O-IOE bait.

An anomaly can indicate:

\text{bad datum}
\;|\;
\text{bad category}
\;|\;
\text{missing dimension}
\;|\;
\boxed{\text{better ontology}}.

Indigo Hammer should therefore never merely discard the odd shard. Hand it to PTERO and ask which direction is up?

